inference_trace:
Approach: strip the parentheses, try every split of the digits into a left part and a right part, and pair up every valid number each part can represent.

For a digit string `t`, the valid numbers are:

- **The whole string as an integer.** This is valid if `t` has length 1 or does not start with `'0'`.
- **A decimal with the point after position `i`** (for each `i` from 1 to `len-1`):
  - The integer part `t[0..i)` is valid if `i == 1` or `t[0] != '0'`.
  - The fractional part must not end with `'0'`.

For each split, output `"(" + a + ", " + b + ")"` for every valid `a` from the left part and every valid `b` from the right part.

The examples show a trailing space inside the quotes, e.g. `"(1, 3) "`. That is a formatting artifact of the statement; the real LeetCode output has no trailing space, so the code doesn't add one.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> gen(const string& t) {
        vector<string> r;
        int n = t.size();
        if (n == 1 || t[0] != '0') r.push_back(t);
        if (t[n-1] == '0') return r;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (i > 1 && t[0] == '0') break;
            r.push_back(t.substr(0, i) + "." + t.substr(i));
        }
        return r;
    }
    vector<string> ambiguousCoordinates(string s) {
        string d = s.substr(1, s.size() - 2);
        vector<string> res;
        int n = d.size();
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            auto L = gen(d.substr(0, i));
            auto R = gen(d.substr(i));
            for (auto& a : L)
                for (auto& b : R)
                    res.push_back("(" + a + ", " + b + ")");
        }
        return res;
    }
};
```

