inference_trace:
Sort the array so that every possible child value comes before its parent. Let `dp[v]` be the number of trees whose root is `v`.

- Each value can stand alone as a single-node tree, so `dp[v]` starts at 1.
- For each smaller value `a` that divides `v`, let `b = v / a`. If `b` is also in the array, add `dp[a] * dp[b]` to `dp[v]`.
- Iterating over all ordered pairs `(a, b)` this way counts left and right children separately, which matches the problem (`[10,2,5]` and `[10,5,2]` are different trees).

The answer is the sum of `dp` over all values, taken modulo 1e9+7.

Use `long long` for the products to avoid overflow. With n ≤ 1000, this runs in O(n²).

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int numFactoredBinaryTrees(vector<int>& arr) {
        const long long MOD = 1e9 + 7;
        sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());
        unordered_map<int, long long> dp;
        long long ans = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < (int)arr.size(); i++) {
            long long cur = 1;
            int v = arr[i];
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                int a = arr[j];
                if (v % a == 0) {
                    int b = v / a;
                    auto it = dp.find(b);
                    if (it != dp.end()) {
                        cur = (cur + dp[a] * it->second) % MOD;
                    }
                }
            }
            dp[v] = cur;
            ans = (ans + cur) % MOD;
        }
        return (int)ans;
    }
};
```

