Simulate the expression with a stack, which handles operator precedence.

1. Push `n` onto the stack.
2. Walk through `n-1` down to `1`, cycling the operator index through `*`, `/`, `+`, `-` in that order:
   - For `*`, replace the top of the stack with `top * i`.
   - For `/`, replace the top with `top / i`.
   - For `+`, push `i`.
   - For `-`, push `-i`.
3. The answer is the sum of everything on the stack.

All values stay positive before any division is applied, so C++ truncating division matches floor division here.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int clumsy(int n) {
        vector<long long> st;
        st.push_back(n);
        int op = 0;
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 1; --i) {
            if (op == 0) st.back() *= i;
            else if (op == 1) st.back() /= i;
            else if (op == 2) st.push_back(i);
            else st.push_back(-i);
            op = (op + 1) % 4;
        }
        long long s = 0;
        for (auto v : st) s += v;
        return (int)s;
    }
};
```

