inference_trace:
The maximum OR over all subsets is the OR of every element, because adding an element to a subset can never turn a bit off. So we only need to count the non-empty subsets whose OR equals that total.

With at most 16 elements there are at most 2^16 subsets, so we can enumerate them all:

- Do a DFS over the indices. At each index, either include the element (OR it into the running value) or skip it.
- When we reach the end, count the subset if its OR equals the total.
- The empty subset has OR 0. Since every `nums[i] >= 1`, the total is non-zero, so the empty subset is never counted by mistake.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countMaxOrSubsets(vector<int>& nums) {
        int target = 0;
        for (int x : nums) target |= x;
        int n = nums.size(), cnt = 0;
        function<void(int,int)> dfs = [&](int i, int cur) {
            if (i == n) { if (cur == target) cnt++; return; }
            dfs(i + 1, cur | nums[i]);
            dfs(i + 1, cur);
        };
        dfs(0, 0);
        return cnt;
    }
};
```

