The string has at most 100 characters, so an O(n²) brute force is fast enough.

1. For each start index `i`, extend the substring to the right one character at a time.
2. Stop as soon as a non-vowel appears, since no longer substring from `i` can be valid.
3. Track which vowels have been seen with a 5-bit mask (one bit per vowel).
4. Whenever the mask equals 31 (all five bits set), the current substring is a vowel substring, so increment the count.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countVowelSubstrings(string word) {
        int n = word.size(), cnt = 0;
        string v = "aeiou";
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int mask = 0;
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                size_t p = v.find(word[j]);
                if (p == string::npos) break;
                mask |= 1 << p;
                if (mask == 31) cnt++;
            }
        }
        return cnt;
    }
};
```

