The two operations let us rearrange a string freely, but they never create or remove a character, and they never change which counts exist. So two strings are close exactly when these conditions all hold:

1. **Same length.** Neither operation changes the length.
2. **Same set of characters.** Operation 1 only reorders existing characters. Operation 2 only swaps between characters that already exist. So no new letter can appear and no existing letter can disappear.
3. **Same multiset of frequencies.** Operation 2 swaps the counts of two existing letters, so any permutation of counts among the present letters is reachable. Operation 1 then puts the characters in any order.

To check this:
- Count each letter's frequency in both strings.
- Compare which letters are present in each.
- Sort both frequency arrays and compare them.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool closeStrings(string word1, string word2) {
        if (word1.size() != word2.size()) return false;
        vector<int> a(26, 0), b(26, 0);
        for (char c : word1) a[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : word2) b[c - 'a']++;
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if ((a[i] > 0) != (b[i] > 0)) return false;
        }
        sort(a.begin(), a.end());
        sort(b.begin(), b.end());
        return a == b;
    }
};
```

