Walk through the array once. Keep `last`, the end of the most recently added range of indices.

1. When `nums[j] == key`, every index from `max(j-k, last+1)` to `min(n-1, j+k)` is k-distant.
2. Add those indices to the result, then set `last` to the end of that range.
3. Starting each new range at `last+1` means no index is added twice.
4. Ranges are processed left to right, so the output is already in increasing order.

Each index is added at most once, so the scan runs in O(n) time.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> findKDistantIndices(vector<int>& nums, int key, int k) {
        vector<int> res;
        int n = nums.size();
        int last = -1;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (nums[j] == key) {
                int s = max(j - k, last + 1);
                int e = min(n - 1, j + k);
                for (int i = s; i <= e; i++) res.push_back(i);
                if (e > last) last = e;
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
};
```

