Use DP over stones: `dp[i][k]` is true if the frog can land on stone `i` with a last jump of `k` units.

**Key bound.** The frog makes at most one jump per stone it lands on, and each jump grows by at most 1. So the jump that lands on stone `i` is at most `i`. This means `k` only needs to range up to `n`, and the table is at most 2001×2001 booleans, about 4M entries, which fits.

**Transition.** Process stones in order. For each pair `j < i`, let `k = stones[i] - stones[j]`.
- If `k > j + 1`, the jump is too long to have come from stone `j`, so skip it.
- Otherwise, `dp[i][k]` is true if stone `j` was reached with a last jump of `k-1`, `k`, or `k+1`, i.e. `dp[j][k-1] || dp[j][k] || dp[j][k+1]`.

**Base case.** Mark `dp[0][0] = true`. From stone 0 only a jump of `k = 1` is allowed. The check reads `dp[0][k-1] = dp[0][0]`, which is true. For any larger `k` at `j = 0`, the condition `k > j + 1` already rejects it.

**Shortcut.** If any adjacent gap `stones[i] - stones[i-1]` exceeds `i`, return false immediately.

**Complexity.** The double loop is O(n²), about 4M operations, which is fine.

**Answer.** Stone `n-1` is reachable if any entry in its row is true.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool canCross(vector<int>& stones) {
        int n = stones.size();
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if ((long long)stones[i] - stones[i-1] > i) return false;
        }
        vector<vector<char>> dp(n, vector<char>(n + 2, 0));
        dp[0][0] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                long long kk = (long long)stones[i] - stones[j];
                if (kk > j + 1) continue;
                int k = (int)kk;
                if (dp[j][k-1] || dp[j][k] || dp[j][k+1]) {
                    dp[i][k] = 1;
                    if (i == n - 1) return true;
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
};
```

