inference_trace:
Use prefix sums so any row, column, or diagonal segment sum is O(1), then try square sizes from largest to smallest.

**Prefix sums**
- `row[i][j+1]` is the sum of `grid[i][0..j]`.
- `col[i+1][j]` is the sum of `grid[0..i][j]`.
- `d1[i+1][j+1] = d1[i][j] + grid[i][j]` accumulates along the main diagonal direction.
- `d2[i+1][j] = d2[i][j+1] + grid[i][j]` accumulates along the anti-diagonal direction (stored with a column shift).

**Segment sums for a square with top-left `(i, j)` and size `k`**
- Row `r`: `row[r][j+k] - row[r][j]`.
- Column `c`: `col[i+k][c] - col[i][c]`.
- Main diagonal, cells `(i+t, j+t)`: `d1[i+k][j+k] - d1[i][j]`.
- Anti-diagonal, cells `(i+t, j+k-1-t)` for t = 0..k-1:
  - The prefix is defined as `d2[i+1][j] = d2[i][j+1] + grid[i][j]`.
  - The cell `(i, j+k-1)` corresponds to the index `d2[i+1][j+k-1]`, which extends `d2[i][j+k]`.
  - The last cell `(i+k-1, j)` gives `d2[i+k][j]`.
  - So the sum is `d2[i+k][j] - d2[i][j+k]`.

**Search**
For k from `min(m, n)` down to 2, check every position. If all row sums, column sums, and both diagonal sums match, return k. If nothing is found, return 1.

**Complexity and overflow**
The work is O(m·n·min(m, n)²), which is fine for 50×50. Sums can reach about 50·10⁶ = 5·10⁷, so `int` is safe, but `long long` is used anyway.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int largestMagicSquare(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
        int m = grid.size(), n = grid[0].size();
        vector<vector<long long>> row(m, vector<long long>(n+1,0)), col(m+1, vector<long long>(n,0));
        vector<vector<long long>> d1(m+1, vector<long long>(n+1,0)), d2(m+1, vector<long long>(n+2,0));
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                row[i][j+1] = row[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                col[i+1][j] = col[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                d1[i+1][j+1] = d1[i][j] + grid[i][j];
                d2[i+1][j] = d2[i][j+1] + grid[i][j];
            }
        for (int k = min(m, n); k >= 2; k--) {
            for (int i = 0; i + k <= m; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j + k <= n; j++) {
                    long long s = d1[i+k][j+k] - d1[i][j];
                    if (d2[i+k][j] - d2[i][j+k] != s) continue;
                    bool ok = true;
                    for (int r = i; r < i + k && ok; r++)
                        if (row[r][j+k] - row[r][j] != s) ok = false;
                    for (int c = j; c < j + k && ok; c++)
                        if (col[i+k][c] - col[i][c] != s) ok = false;
                    if (ok) return k;
                }
            }
        }
        return 1;
    }
};
```

