inference_trace:
Sort the words by length so every predecessor is processed before any word that could follow it. Then use DP with a hash map:

- `dp[w]` is the length of the longest chain ending at word `w`.
- For each word `w`, try deleting each character to get a candidate predecessor `p`.
- If `p` is already in the map, set `dp[w] = max(dp[w], dp[p] + 1)`. Otherwise `dp[w]` starts at 1.
- The answer is the largest `dp` value seen.

With up to 1000 words of length at most 16, each word produces at most 16 deletions, so this runs quickly.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int longestStrChain(vector<string>& words) {
        sort(words.begin(), words.end(), [](const string& a, const string& b){ return a.size() < b.size(); });
        unordered_map<string,int> dp;
        int ans = 0;
        for (auto& w : words) {
            int best = 1;
            for (size_t i = 0; i < w.size(); i++) {
                string p = w.substr(0, i) + w.substr(i + 1);
                auto it = dp.find(p);
                if (it != dp.end()) best = max(best, it->second + 1);
            }
            dp[w] = max(dp[w], best);
            ans = max(ans, best);
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
```

