inference_trace:
Fix each point `i` as an anchor, then compute the direction from `i` to every other point `j`. Points that share the same direction from `i` all lie on one line through `i`.

1. For each pair, take `dx = xj - xi` and `dy = yj - yi`.
2. Reduce the direction to a canonical form:
   - Divide both by `g = gcd(|dx|, |dy|)`.
   - Flip signs so that `dx > 0`, or `dx == 0` and `dy > 0`.
3. Encode the reduced pair as a single integer key and count keys in a hash map.
4. The best line through `i` has (largest count + 1) points. Take the maximum over all anchors.

The answer is at least 1 when there is a single point.

Key encoding: after reduction `|dy| <= 2·10^4`, so `dx * 100000 + dy` is unique for each direction and fits in a `long long`.

Complexity: O(n²) pairs, each with a gcd and a hash-map update. With n = 300 this is well within limits.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxPoints(vector<vector<int>>& points) {
        int n = points.size();
        int best = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            unordered_map<long long, int> cnt;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                int dx = points[j][0] - points[i][0];
                int dy = points[j][1] - points[i][1];
                int g = std::gcd(abs(dx), abs(dy));
                dx /= g; dy /= g;
                if (dx < 0 || (dx == 0 && dy < 0)) { dx = -dx; dy = -dy; }
                long long key = (long long)dx * 100000LL + dy;
                int c = ++cnt[key];
                best = max(best, c + 1);
            }
        }
        return best;
    }
};
```

