Use a bitmask DP over a base-3 encoding of the slots.

**State.** Each slot holds 0, 1 or 2 numbers, so the fill levels of all slots form a base-3 number. With at most 9 slots there are at most 3^9 = 19683 states.

**Key observation.** The total count of placed numbers in a state is the sum of its base-3 digits. Call that count `i`. We always place numbers in array order, so the next number to place is `nums[i]`.

**Transition.** Let `dp[mask]` be the best AND sum for that state, with `dp[0] = 0` and all other states starting at -1 (unreachable).
- Iterate `mask` in increasing order.
- If the digit count `i` reaches `n`, the state is complete; use it to update the answer.
- Otherwise, for each slot `j` whose digit is below 2, move to `mask + 3^j`.
- The gain for that move is `nums[i] & (j+1)`.

Adding `3^j` always gives a larger mask, so iterating in increasing order processes every state after all its predecessors.

**Complexity.** Each of the up to 19683 states tries 9 slots, so the work is about 177k transitions.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maximumANDSum(vector<int>& nums, int numSlots) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int total = 1;
        vector<int> pw(numSlots + 1);
        pw[0] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i <= numSlots; i++) pw[i] = pw[i-1] * 3;
        total = pw[numSlots];
        vector<int> dp(total, -1);
        dp[0] = 0;
        int ans = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < total; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
            int cnt = 0, t = mask;
            for (int j = 0; j < numSlots; j++) { cnt += t % 3; t /= 3; }
            if (cnt >= n) { ans = max(ans, dp[mask]); continue; }
            t = mask;
            for (int j = 0; j < numSlots; j++) {
                int d = t % 3; t /= 3;
                if (d < 2) {
                    int nm = mask + pw[j];
                    dp[nm] = max(dp[nm], dp[mask] + (nums[cnt] & (j + 1)));
                }
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
```

