inference_trace:
For each prefix length `len = i + 1`, track two tables:

- `cnt[x]`: how many times value `x` has appeared so far.
- `freq[f]`: how many distinct values appear exactly `f` times.
- `maxf`: the largest count of any value so far.

When `nums[i]` arrives, its old count `c` is moved to `c + 1`: decrement `freq[c]` (if `c > 0`), increment `freq[c + 1]`, and update `maxf`.

The prefix is valid if removing one element can equalize all counts. That happens in exactly three cases:

1. **`maxf == 1`**: every value appears once. Removing any one element leaves all remaining values at count 1 (or nothing left, which also counts).
2. **`freq[maxf] * maxf == len - 1`**: all values except one are at `maxf`, and the leftover single element is a value that appears once. Removing it equalizes the rest. This includes the case where the whole prefix is one value repeated, since then `len - 1` equals that value's count minus one.
3. **`freq[maxf] == 1` and `freq[maxf - 1] * (maxf - 1) + maxf == len`**: exactly one value is at `maxf` and all others are at `maxf - 1`. Removing one occurrence of the top value equalizes everything.

Whenever any case holds, record `len` as a candidate answer and return the largest one.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxEqualFreq(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> cnt(100002,0), freq(100002,0);
        int maxf=0, ans=0;
        for(int i=0;i<(int)nums.size();i++){
            int x=nums[i];
            int c=cnt[x];
            if(c>0) freq[c]--;
            cnt[x]=c+1;
            freq[c+1]++;
            maxf=max(maxf,c+1);
            int len=i+1;
            bool ok=false;
            if(maxf==1) ok=true;
            else if((long long)freq[maxf]*maxf==len-1) ok=true;
            else if(freq[maxf]==1 && (long long)freq[maxf-1]*(maxf-1)+maxf==len) ok=true;
            if(ok) ans=len;
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
```

