inference_trace:
Approach:

1. Mark each broken letter in a 26-entry boolean array. Only lowercase letters are marked, so any stray spaces in the input are ignored.
2. Scan `text` one character at a time, keeping a flag `ok` that says whether the current word is still typeable.
3. When a space is reached, a word has just ended:
   - If it had at least one character and `ok` is still true, count it.
   - Reset the length and set `ok` back to true for the next word.
4. For any other character, increase the word length and set `ok` to false if the letter is broken.
5. After the loop, check the last word the same way.

Counting only words with a non-zero length means extra spaces (such as a trailing one) are handled safely.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int canBeTypedWords(string text, string brokenLetters) {
        bool broken[26] = {false};
        for (char c : brokenLetters) if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') broken[c - 'a'] = true;
        int count = 0;
        bool ok = true;
        int len = 0;
        for (char c : text) {
            if (c == ' ') {
                if (len > 0 && ok) count++;
                ok = true;
                len = 0;
            } else {
                len++;
                if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' && broken[c - 'a']) ok = false;
            }
        }
        if (len > 0 && ok) count++;
        return count;
    }
};
```

