inference_trace:
Use prefix sums and handle the two possible orders separately.

1. Build a prefix-sum array `p`, where `p[i]` is the sum of the first `i` elements. The sum of a subarray of length `L` ending just before index `i` is then `p[i] - p[i-L]`.

2. Define a helper `f(L, M)` for the case where the length-`L` subarray comes before the length-`M` subarray:
   - Iterate `i` from `L + M` to `n`, where `i` is the end (exclusive) of the length-`M` subarray.
   - Keep `bestL`, the maximum sum of any length-`L` subarray ending at or before `i - M`. Update it with `p[i-M] - p[i-M-L]` at each step.
   - The candidate answer is `bestL + (p[i] - p[i-M])`; track the maximum.

3. The answer is `max(f(firstLen, secondLen), f(secondLen, firstLen))`, covering both orders.

The whole computation is O(n) time and O(n) space.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxSumTwoNoOverlap(vector<int>& nums, int firstLen, int secondLen) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> p(n + 1, 0);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) p[i + 1] = p[i] + nums[i];
        auto f = [&](int L, int M) {
            int bestL = 0, res = 0;
            for (int i = L + M; i <= n; i++) {
                bestL = max(bestL, p[i - M] - p[i - M - L]);
                res = max(res, bestL + p[i] - p[i - M]);
            }
            return res;
        };
        return max(f(firstLen, secondLen), f(secondLen, firstLen));
    }
};
```

