inference_trace:
Approach: for each clean node, find which infected nodes can reach it without passing through any other infected node. If exactly one infected node `u` reaches it, then removing `u` saves that node.

1. Mark all nodes in `initial` as infected.
2. For each infected node `u`, run a BFS/DFS over clean nodes only (never entering another infected node). Every clean node `v` reached is recorded as reachable from `u`.
3. For each clean node `v`, if exactly one infected node reached it, add 1 to that node's "saved" count.
4. Return the infected node with the largest saved count. Ties go to the smallest index, so sort `initial` first and only replace the answer on a strictly larger count.

Why this is correct:
- If two or more infected nodes reach `v` through clean paths, removing one of them still leaves `v` infected.
- If only `u` reaches `v` this way, removing `u` saves `v`. Any path from another infected node to `v` would pass through an infected node first, and the first such node's clean suffix would then reach `v`, contradicting uniqueness.

Complexity: each BFS is O(n²) on the adjacency matrix, so the total is O(k·n²). With n = 300 this is about 27M operations, which is fine.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minMalwareSpread(vector<vector<int>>& graph, vector<int>& initial) {
        int n = graph.size();
        vector<int> infected(n, 0);
        for (int x : initial) infected[x] = 1;
        vector<int> cnt(n, 0), who(n, -1);
        for (int u : initial) {
            vector<int> seen(n, 0);
            vector<int> st = {u};
            seen[u] = 1;
            while (!st.empty()) {
                int x = st.back(); st.pop_back();
                for (int y = 0; y < n; y++) {
                    if (graph[x][y] && !seen[y] && !infected[y]) {
                        seen[y] = 1;
                        st.push_back(y);
                        cnt[y]++;
                        who[y] = u;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        vector<int> saved(n, 0);
        for (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {
            if (!infected[v] && cnt[v] == 1) saved[who[v]]++;
        }
        sort(initial.begin(), initial.end());
        int best = initial[0];
        for (int u : initial) {
            if (saved[u] > saved[best]) best = u;
        }
        return best;
    }
};
```

