A breadth-first search (BFS) solves this cleanly.

1. If the tree is empty, the minimum depth is 0.
2. Otherwise, visit the tree level by level, starting from the root at depth 1.
3. The first leaf we reach (a node with no left and no right child) is on the shallowest level that has a leaf. Its level number is the answer.

BFS avoids the deep recursion a DFS would need on a skewed tree. The tree can have up to 10^5 nodes, so a chain of that length could overflow the call stack.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minDepth(TreeNode* root) {
        if (!root) return 0;
        std::queue<TreeNode*> q;
        q.push(root);
        int depth = 0;
        while (!q.empty()) {
            depth++;
            int sz = q.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < sz; i++) {
                TreeNode* n = q.front(); q.pop();
                if (!n->left && !n->right) return depth;
                if (n->left) q.push(n->left);
                if (n->right) q.push(n->right);
            }
        }
        return depth;
    }
};
```

