inference_trace:
Use memoized recursion. From `n`, the best plan is to eat single oranges only until the count is divisible by 2 or by 3, then apply the big reduction:

- `f(n) = 1 + min(n % 2 + f(n / 2), n % 3 + f(n / 3))`
- Base cases: `f(0) = 0` and `f(1) = 1`.

Here `n % 2` (or `n % 3`) is the number of single-orange days needed to reach a multiple of 2 (or 3), and the extra `1` is the day spent on the halving (or thirding) move.

Only values of the form `n / (2^a · 3^b)` are ever visited, so the number of distinct states is small (roughly O(log² n)). Storing results in an `unordered_map` makes the recursion fast even for `n` up to 2·10⁹.

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    unordered_map<int,int> memo;
    int minDays(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        auto it = memo.find(n);
        if (it != memo.end()) return it->second;
        int r = 1 + min(n % 2 + minDays(n / 2), n % 3 + minDays(n / 3));
        memo[n] = r;
        return r;
    }
};
```

