inference_trace:
The approach is a bitmask DP over subsets of tasks. For each subset `mask`, store the best pair `(sessions, lastTime)`:

- `sessions` is the minimum number of sessions used to finish exactly the tasks in `mask`.
- `lastTime` is the time already spent in the current (last) session.
- Pairs are compared lexicographically: fewer sessions is better, and for equal sessions a smaller `lastTime` is better.

**Transition.** From `dp[mask] = (s, t)`, try adding each task `i` not yet in `mask`:

- If `t + tasks[i] <= sessionTime`, the task fits in the current session, giving `(s, t + tasks[i])`.
- Otherwise it starts a new session, giving `(s + 1, tasks[i])`.

**Base case.** `dp[0] = (1, 0)`: one session is open with no time used.

**Answer.** `dp[full].first`, where `full` is the mask containing every task.

**Complexity.** There are 2^14 masks and 14 tasks per mask, so about 2^14 · 14 transitions. That is easily fast enough.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minSessions(vector<int>& tasks, int sessionTime) {
        int n = tasks.size();
        int full = 1 << n;
        vector<pair<int,int>> dp(full, {INT_MAX, INT_MAX});
        dp[0] = {1, 0};
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask].first == INT_MAX) continue;
            auto [s, t] = dp[mask];
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if (mask & (1 << i)) continue;
                int nm = mask | (1 << i);
                pair<int,int> cand;
                if (t + tasks[i] <= sessionTime) cand = {s, t + tasks[i]};
                else cand = {s + 1, tasks[i]};
                if (cand < dp[nm]) dp[nm] = cand;
            }
        }
        return dp[full - 1].first;
    }
};
```

