inference_trace:
Use a monotonic stack and make two passes over the array to handle the circular wrap.

1. Start with `res` filled with `-1` and an empty stack of indices. The values at those indices are kept in decreasing order.
2. Iterate `i` from `0` to `2n - 1` and look at `x = nums[i % n]`.
3. While the stack is not empty and the value at its top index is less than `x`, pop that index and set its answer to `x`.
4. Push `i` onto the stack only during the first pass (`i < n`). The second pass exists only to resolve elements whose next greater number wraps around.
5. Indices still on the stack at the end have no greater number, so they keep `-1`.

Each index is pushed and popped at most once, so the solution runs in O(n) time with O(n) extra space.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> nextGreaterElements(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> res(n, -1);
        vector<int> st;
        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
            int x = nums[i % n];
            while (!st.empty() && nums[st.back()] < x) {
                res[st.back()] = x;
                st.pop_back();
            }
            if (i < n) st.push_back(i);
        }
        return res;
    }
};
```

