inference_trace:
Use a union-find (DSU) over the people.

For each request `(u, v)`:
1. Find the roots `pu` and `pv`.
2. If they are already in the same group, the request succeeds.
3. Otherwise, check every restriction `(x, y)`:
   - Let `px = find(x)` and `py = find(y)`.
   - Merging would connect `x` and `y` if `{px, py} == {pu, pv}`.
   - If any restriction matches this, the request fails.
4. If no restriction is violated, union the two groups and mark the request successful.

Each request scans all restrictions, so the total cost is about 1000 × 1000 = 10^6 operations times the near-constant `find` cost, which is fine.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> p;
    int find(int x){ while(p[x]!=x){p[x]=p[p[x]];x=p[x];} return x; }
    vector<bool> friendRequests(int n, vector<vector<int>>& restrictions, vector<vector<int>>& requests) {
        p.resize(n);
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++)p[i]=i;
        vector<bool> res;
        for(auto& r:requests){
            int pu=find(r[0]), pv=find(r[1]);
            if(pu==pv){res.push_back(true);continue;}
            bool ok=true;
            for(auto& s:restrictions){
                int px=find(s[0]), py=find(s[1]);
                if((px==pu&&py==pv)||(px==pv&&py==pu)){ok=false;break;}
            }
            if(ok)p[pu]=pv;
            res.push_back(ok);
        }
        return res;
    }
};
```

