Plan: in the constructor, group indices by value in a hash map. Then `pick` chooses uniformly among the stored indices for `target`. That is O(1) per pick after O(n) preprocessing.

- Reservoir sampling would also work. It costs O(n) per pick, so 10^4 picks over 2·10^4 elements is about 2·10^8 operations.
- That is likely fine but slower, so the map approach is the better choice.
- For randomness, use `mt19937` with `uniform_int_distribution` so each index is equally likely.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    unordered_map<int, vector<int>> mp;
    mt19937 rng;
    Solution(vector<int>& nums) : rng(random_device{}()) {
        for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); i++) mp[nums[i]].push_back(i);
    }

    int pick(int target) {
        auto& v = mp[target];
        uniform_int_distribution<int> d(0, (int)v.size() - 1);
        return v[d(rng)];
    }
};
```

