Two axis-aligned rectangles overlap with positive area exactly when their projections overlap with positive length on both axes.

- On the x-axis, the overlap has positive length when `max(left edges) < min(right edges)`, i.e. `max(x1, a1) < min(x2, a2)`.
- The same condition must hold on the y-axis.

Touching edges or corners give an overlap length of exactly 0, so the strict `<` correctly returns false for them.

There is no overflow risk: the code only compares coordinates and never subtracts them. Values are bounded by roughly 1e9 in magnitude, which fits in `int`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isRectangleOverlap(vector<int>& rec1, vector<int>& rec2) {
        return max(rec1[0], rec2[0]) < min(rec1[2], rec2[2]) &&
               max(rec1[1], rec2[1]) < min(rec1[3], rec2[3]);
    }
};
```

