Treat the array as a path: each value is a node and each pair is an edge.

1. Build an adjacency map from each value to its neighbours.
2. The two endpoints of the array are the only values with exactly one neighbour. Pick one as the start.
3. Walk the path. At each step, move to the neighbour that is not the previous element.

Values can be negative, including `-100000`, so no value can serve as a "no previous element" sentinel. Instead, compare positions:
- For `i >= 2`, the previous element is `res[i-2]`.
- The second element is simply the start's only neighbour.

This runs in O(n) time and O(n) space.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> restoreArray(vector<vector<int>>& adjacentPairs) {
        unordered_map<int, vector<int>> adj;
        adj.reserve(adjacentPairs.size() * 4);
        for (auto& p : adjacentPairs) {
            adj[p[0]].push_back(p[1]);
            adj[p[1]].push_back(p[0]);
        }
        int n = adjacentPairs.size() + 1;
        int start = 0;
        for (auto& kv : adj) {
            if (kv.second.size() == 1) { start = kv.first; break; }
        }
        vector<int> res(n);
        res[0] = start;
        res[1] = adj[start][0];
        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            auto& nb = adj[res[i-1]];
            res[i] = (nb[0] == res[i-2]) ? nb[1] : nb[0];
        }
        return res;
    }
};
```

