Use bottom-up dynamic programming. Let `dp[len][i][j]` be true when the substring of `s1` starting at `i` with length `len` can be scrambled into the substring of `s2` starting at `j` with the same length.

**Base case (`len == 1`):** `dp[1][i][j]` is true exactly when `s1[i] == s2[j]`.

**Transition (`len > 1`):** try every split point `k` from 1 to `len - 1`. The substrings match if either case holds:

- **No swap:** `dp[k][i][j] && dp[len-k][i+k][j+k]`. The first `k` characters of `s1` map to the first `k` of `s2`, and the rest map to the rest.
- **Swap:** `dp[k][i][j+len-k] && dp[len-k][i+k][j]`. The first `k` characters of `s1` map to the last `k` of `s2`, and the remaining `len-k` map to the first `len-k`.

The answer is `dp[n][0][0]`.

**Complexity:** O(n^4) time, which is about 810,000 steps for n = 30. Space is O(n^3).

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isScramble(string s1, string s2) {
        int n = s1.size();
        if (n != (int)s2.size()) return false;
        static bool dp[31][31][31];
        for (int l = 0; l <= n; l++)
            for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++)
                for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++)
                    dp[l][i][j] = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
                dp[1][i][j] = (s1[i] == s2[j]);
        for (int len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
            for (int i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j + len <= n; j++) {
                    bool ok = false;
                    for (int k = 1; k < len && !ok; k++) {
                        if (dp[k][i][j] && dp[len - k][i + k][j + k]) ok = true;
                        else if (dp[k][i][j + len - k] && dp[len - k][i + k][j]) ok = true;
                    }
                    dp[len][i][j] = ok;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n][0][0];
    }
};
```

