Treat the grid as one flat array of length `m * n`, read in row-major order. One shift moves every element one position forward in this flat array, and the last element wraps to the front. So `k` shifts move the element at flat index `idx` to index `(idx + k) % (m * n)`.

Steps:
1. Let `total = m * n` and reduce `k` to `k % total`.
2. For each cell `(i, j)`, compute its flat index `idx = i * n + j`.
3. Compute the new flat index `ni = (idx + k) % total`.
4. Place the value at row `ni / n`, column `ni % n` of the result grid.

This runs in O(m·n) time and uses O(m·n) extra space for the result.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> shiftGrid(vector<vector<int>>& grid, int k) {
        int m = grid.size(), n = grid[0].size();
        int total = m * n;
        k %= total;
        vector<vector<int>> res(m, vector<int>(n));
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                int idx = i * n + j;
                int ni = (idx + k) % total;
                res[ni / n][ni % n] = grid[i][j];
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
};
```

