Use a greedy construction. At each step, write the letter that has more remaining, unless the last two characters are already that letter; in that case write the other letter.

- Track the counts of `'a'` and `'b'` still to place.
- Let `n` be the current length of the string.
- **Forced switch:** if the last two characters are both `'a'`, write `'b'`; if both are `'b'`, write `'a'`.
- **Normal case:** otherwise, write `'a'` if `a >= b`, else `'b'`.

The problem guarantees a valid answer exists, so this greedy never runs out of the letter it is forced to write.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string strWithout3a3b(int a, int b) {
        string s;
        while (a > 0 || b > 0) {
            int n = s.size();
            bool writeA;
            if (n >= 2 && s[n-1] == s[n-2]) {
                writeA = (s[n-1] == 'b');
            } else {
                writeA = (a >= b);
            }
            if (writeA) { s += 'a'; a--; }
            else { s += 'b'; b--; }
        }
        return s;
    }
};
```

