The approach is a memoized search over the current state of the tournament.

**State.** A state is `(m, a, b)`:
- `m` is the number of players left in the row.
- `a < b` are the 0-indexed positions of the two best players in the current row.

**Base case.** In each round, position `i` is paired with position `m-1-i`. So if `a + b == m - 1`, the two players meet in this round, and the answer for this state is `{1, 1}`.

**Transition.** Otherwise, enumerate the outcomes of the `m/2` pairs with a bitmask:
- Any pair that contains `a` or `b` is forced: that best player wins.
- Every other pair can go either way, chosen by the corresponding bit.
- If `m` is odd, the middle player always advances.

Survivors keep their original relative order. So the new positions of the two best players are simply the number of survivors before each of them. We recurse on `((m+1)/2, newA, newB)`. Over all outcomes, we take the minimum of the earliest rounds and the maximum of the latest rounds, then add 1.

**Complexity.** `m` at least halves each round, so there are few distinct `m` values. For each `m` there are at most about 28² position pairs, and at most 2¹⁴ masks per state. Memoization keeps the total work small.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int memoE[30][30][30], memoL[30][30][30];
    bool vis[30][30][30];

    pair<int,int> dfs(int m, int a, int b) {
        if (a + b == m - 1) return {1, 1};
        if (vis[m][a][b]) return {memoE[m][a][b], memoL[m][a][b]};
        vis[m][a][b] = true;
        int half = m / 2;
        int best = INT_MAX, worst = 0;
        int nm = (m + 1) / 2;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << half); mask++) {
            bool alive[30] = {false};
            if (m % 2 == 1) alive[m / 2] = true;
            for (int i = 0; i < half; i++) {
                int j = m - 1 - i;
                if (i == a || i == b) alive[i] = true;
                else if (j == a || j == b) alive[j] = true;
                else if (mask >> i & 1) alive[i] = true;
                else alive[j] = true;
            }
            int na = 0, nb = 0;
            for (int k = 0; k < a; k++) if (alive[k]) na++;
            for (int k = 0; k < b; k++) if (alive[k]) nb++;
            auto r = dfs(nm, na, nb);
            best = min(best, r.first + 1);
            worst = max(worst, r.second + 1);
        }
        memoE[m][a][b] = best;
        memoL[m][a][b] = worst;
        return {best, worst};
    }

    vector<int> earliestAndLatest(int n, int firstPlayer, int secondPlayer) {
        memset(vis, 0, sizeof(vis));
        auto r = dfs(n, firstPlayer - 1, secondPlayer - 1);
        return {r.first, r.second};
    }
};
```

