inference_trace:
Approach: a valid tree has exactly one root, every other node has exactly one parent, and the root reaches all `n` nodes.

1. **Count parents.** For each node `i`, add 1 to the in-degree of `leftChild[i]` and `rightChild[i]` when they are not `-1`. If any node ends up with in-degree greater than 1, it has two parents, so return `false`.
2. **Find the root.** Exactly one node must have in-degree 0. If there are zero or more than one such nodes, return `false`.
3. **Check connectivity.** Run a BFS from the root and count the nodes visited. If a node is reached a second time, return `false`. The answer is `true` only if the count equals `n`.

This also rules out cycles. With one root and every other node having in-degree 1, the graph has exactly `n - 1` edges. If the root reaches all `n` nodes using those edges, the structure is a tree.

Time and space are both O(n).

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool validateBinaryTreeNodes(int n, vector<int>& leftChild, vector<int>& rightChild) {
        vector<int> indeg(n, 0);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (leftChild[i] != -1 && ++indeg[leftChild[i]] > 1) return false;
            if (rightChild[i] != -1 && ++indeg[rightChild[i]] > 1) return false;
        }
        int root = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (indeg[i] == 0) {
                if (root != -1) return false;
                root = i;
            }
        }
        if (root == -1) return false;
        vector<bool> vis(n, false);
        queue<int> q;
        q.push(root);
        vis[root] = true;
        int cnt = 0;
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int u = q.front(); q.pop();
            cnt++;
            for (int v : {leftChild[u], rightChild[u]}) {
                if (v == -1) continue;
                if (vis[v]) return false;
                vis[v] = true;
                q.push(v);
            }
        }
        return cnt == n;
    }
};
```

