Run a BFS from `beginWord`, treating each word as a node and each single-letter change as an edge.

1. Put every word from `wordList` into a hash set. If `endWord` is not in the set, return 0.
2. Start the BFS queue with `beginWord` at level 1.
3. For each word taken from the queue, try every position and replace that letter with each of `a` to `z`.
4. If the new word equals `endWord`, return the current level + 1.
5. If the new word is still in the set, erase it from the set (so it counts as visited) and push it onto the queue.
6. If the queue empties without reaching `endWord`, return 0.

Complexity: each word is generated and checked about `L * 26` times, where `L` is the word length (at most 10). With at most 5000 words, this is fast enough.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int ladderLength(string beginWord, string endWord, vector<string>& wordList) {
        unordered_set<string> dict(wordList.begin(), wordList.end());
        if (!dict.count(endWord)) return 0;
        dict.erase(beginWord);
        queue<string> q;
        q.push(beginWord);
        int level = 1;
        while (!q.empty()) {
            int sz = q.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < sz; i++) {
                string w = q.front(); q.pop();
                for (int p = 0; p < (int)w.size(); p++) {
                    char orig = w[p];
                    for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
                        if (c == orig) continue;
                        w[p] = c;
                        if (w == endWord) return level + 1;
                        if (dict.count(w)) {
                            dict.erase(w);
                            q.push(w);
                        }
                    }
                    w[p] = orig;
                }
            }
            level++;
        }
        return 0;
    }
};
```

